[h1]Service Terminal Framework[/h1]

Service Terminal Framework turns the Economy 2 Services Terminal into a shared access point for vanilla services and services added by other mods.

Interacting with a Services Terminal opens a centered service-selection menu with large, easy-to-read buttons. After selecting a custom service, the menu becomes a responsive side navigation. On narrow displays it folds into a compact rail that can be expanded when needed.

The framework retains the original Keen interaction instead of replacing the terminal block or model. Selecting [b]Vanilla services[/b] opens the original Services Terminal screen normally.

[h3]One terminal, one consistent menu, any number of registered mod services.[/h3]

[h2]Features[/h2]

[list]
[*]Centered service-selection menu with wide buttons.
[*]Preserves access to Keen's vanilla Services screen.
[*]Supports multiple services registered by other mods.
[*]Responsive side navigation while a custom service is open.
[*]Compact folding navigation for narrower aspect ratios.
[*]Restores the player's previous HUD state when a service closes.
[*]Preserves the terminal's original secondary interactions and HUD hints.
[*]Falls back to vanilla Services if Rich HUD is unavailable.
[*]Does not replace or modify the Services Terminal block definition or model.
[/list]

[h2]Dependencies[/h2]

[url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=1965654081]Rich HUD Master[/url]

[h2]Mods Using This Framework[/h2]

[url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3765484640]Ship Insurance[/url] adds persistent grid insurance, paid repairs, recovery, remote transport, faction discounts, and economy-driven pricing to the Services Terminal.

[h2]Service Terminal Framework - MODAPI[/h2]

Other mods can register their own service name and client-side interface through the framework's mod-message API.

The API uses only game and standard library types. Provider mods do not require a compile-time reference to the framework assembly, and registration handles either mod load order.

Provider names are sorted alphabetically, while Vanilla services always remains available first.

Full API documentation and example code:
[url=https://github.com/OwendB1/SE-Service-Terminal-Mods/tree/production/ServiceTerminalFramework]Service Terminal Framework GitHub Documentation[/url]

[h2]Common Questions[/h2]

What does this mod add by itself?
It provides the shared Services Terminal menu and keeps Vanilla services available. Additional custom options appear when compatible provider mods are enabled.

Does this replace the Economy 2 Services Terminal?
No. It attaches a reversible interaction proxy to live terminals without replacing their block definitions, models, or original service interaction.

What happens if Rich HUD is unavailable?
The original Vanilla services interaction opens normally.

Can multiple mods use the framework at once?
Yes. Each provider registers a stable ID and display name. Registered services appear together in the same menu.

Does mod load order matter?
No. The registration handshake supports both framework-first and provider-first loading.

Can servers use it?
Yes. Add the framework and its provider mods to the world normally. Clients receive the required Workshop mods through the server.

